"peer_ticker": "JPM",
"peer_disclosure_id": "jpm_2024_1A_D037",
"relationship": "same_topic",
"comparison": "Both Goldman Sachs and JPMorgan Chase discuss the risks associated with their broad client and counterparty bases. GS notes it faces 'enhanced risks' from transacting with a 'broader array of clients and counterparties.' JPMorgan provides specific detail on this breadth, noting it 'routinely executes transactions with clients and counterparties such as corporations, financial institutions, asset managers, hedge funds, securities exchanges and government entities,' and that these relationships expose JPMorgan to 'credit risk' which can result in 'disputes and litigation if a client or counterparty defaults.' While GS focuses on the 'enhanced' nature of the risk from breadth, JPMorgan links this breadth directly to specific credit and litigation outcomes.",
